From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 23:22:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: a four-word load must not read through the register it
 filled

output_move_multiple issued the words of a multi-word move in order.
When the destination is a register group and the source is memory
addressed through one of those registers, the word that lands in the
base register overwrites the address the remaining words are still
being fetched through:

  mov  (r2),r0          mov  (r2),r0
  mov  02(r2),r1   ->   mov  02(r2),r1
  mov  04(r2),r2        mov  06(r2),r3
  mov  06(r2),r3        mov  04(r2),r2

pdp11_expand_operands already copes with two words by choosing which
end to start from, and for two words that is enough: the base is
either the first word or the last.  With four it can be in the middle,
where no order works, so the word that clobbers the base is held back
and emitted last instead.

Only an address without side effects may be permuted: with an
auto-increment source all four words share one rtx and the order is
what advances the pointer.  Such an address cannot overlap a register
destination anyway, since that would be two assignments to the base in
one insn.  And pdp11_legitimate_address_p refuses (MEM (MEM ...)) for
modes wider than a word, so at most one word can match.

Reachable because *movdi_nocc's memory alternative has no earlyclobber
on the destination: seen with -mlra, where the allocator picks r0-r3
for the value and r2 for the address.

From prcoder-1/digger-bk0010's GCC patch for the BK-0010.  The test is
this tree's and runs on the machine: it loads a known 64-bit constant
through a base register forced into the middle of the group and checks
the value that comes back.
---
 gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc                     | 37 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../gcc.target/pdp11/movdi-overlap.c          | 38 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 72 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/movdi-overlap.c

diff --git a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
index 488731b..2cd02fd 100644
--- a/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.cc
@@ -688,7 +688,7 @@ output_move_multiple (rtx *operands)
   rtx adjops[2];
 
   pdp11_action action[2];
-  int i, words;
+  int i, words, last;
 
   words = GET_MODE_BITSIZE (GET_MODE (operands[0])) / 16;
   adjops[1] = gen_rtx_CONST_INT (HImode, words * 2);
@@ -710,9 +710,40 @@ output_move_multiple (rtx *operands)
       output_asm_insn ("sub\t%1,%0", adjops);
     }
 
-  /* Do the words.  */
+  /* Do the words.  If the destination is a register group and the
+     source is memory addressed through one of those registers, the
+     word that lands in the base register has to go last -- otherwise
+     the words after it are fetched through a register already
+     overwritten.  pdp11_expand_operands handles the two-word case by
+     choosing which end to start from, but for four words the base can
+     sit in the middle of the group, where no order works and the
+     emission itself has to be permuted.
+
+     Only an address without side effects may be permuted: with an
+     auto-increment source every word shares one rtx and the order is
+     what advances the pointer.  Such an address cannot overlap a
+     register destination anyway -- that would be a second assignment
+     to the base in one insn, which auto-inc-dec.cc does not create.
+
+     pdp11_legitimate_address_p refuses (MEM (MEM ...)) for modes wider
+     than a word, so the address holds at most one register and at most
+     one word can match.  */
+  last = -1;
+  if (REG_P (operands[0]) && MEM_P (operands[1])
+      && !side_effects_p (XEXP (operands[1], 0)))
+    for (i = 0; i < words; i++)
+      if (REG_P (exops[i][0]) && MEM_P (exops[i][1])
+	  && reg_overlap_mentioned_p (exops[i][0], XEXP (exops[i][1], 0)))
+	{
+	  last = i;
+	  break;
+	}
+
   for (i = 0; i < words; i++)
-    output_asm_insn (singlemove_string (exops[i]), exops[i]);
+    if (i != last)
+      output_asm_insn (singlemove_string (exops[i]), exops[i]);
+  if (last >= 0)
+    output_asm_insn (singlemove_string (exops[last]), exops[last]);
 
   /* Check for increment after.  */
   if (action[0] == inc_after)
diff --git a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/movdi-overlap.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/movdi-overlap.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fc53f36
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.target/pdp11/movdi-overlap.c
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+/* { dg-do run { target pdp11-*-* } } */
+/* { dg-options "-O2 -mlra" } */
+
+/* A four-word load into a register group whose address register sits
+   inside that group.  The word that lands in the base register has to
+   be fetched last: with the words in order, the third one overwrites
+   r2 and the fourth is then read through it.
+
+   pdp11_expand_operands copes with the two-word case by choosing which
+   end to start from; for four words the base can be in the middle,
+   where no order works and output_move_multiple has to permute.  */
+
+static long long mem = 0x0123456789abcdefLL;
+
+/* Volatile, so the address has to be loaded at run time: given the
+   symbol outright the compiler addresses it absolutely and the base
+   register never appears.  */
+static long long *volatile where = &mem;
+
+long long __attribute__((noinline))
+load (void)
+{
+  register long long *p asm ("r2");
+  register long long v asm ("r0");
+
+  /* Through an asm, so the address really is in r2 and nothing may
+     re-materialise it somewhere more convenient.  */
+  asm volatile ("mov\t%1,%0" : "=r" (p) : "r" (where));
+  v = *p;
+  asm volatile ("" : : "r" (v));
+  return v;
+}
+
+int
+main (void)
+{
+  return load () != 0x0123456789abcdefLL;
+}
-- 
